# The human hippocampus supports auditory coding and multisensory integration

This dataset contains raw and preprocessed high-resolution fMRI data from
32 healthy adults performing an audiovisual associative memory task. It
accompanies:

  Raccah, O., et al. (2026). The human hippocampus supports auditory coding
  and multisensory integration. PLOS Biology.

## Participants

32 participants (sub-mm01 through sub-mm32). `participants.tsv` gives, for
each participant, an `included` flag and an `exclusion_reason`. Two
participants (sub-mm04, sub-mm12) were excluded from all analyses reported
in the paper for excessive head motion. Their data are included here in
full so that the exclusion can be independently evaluated.

## Task

Each participant completed 9 runs of the `multisensorymemory` task.

Each run's `events.tsv` contains:

  onset       run-relative onset in seconds
  duration    event duration in seconds
  condition   trial condition label
  stim_path   path of the stimulus file presented on that trial

Inter-trial intervals are coded explicitly as `ITI` events rather than
being left implicit, so each run's timeline is fully reconstructable.

## Acquisition

  T1w   208 x 256 x 256, 1.0 mm isotropic, whole head
  T2w   384 x 384 x 54, 0.438 x 0.438 x 1.8 mm, oblique coronal slab
        prescribed perpendicular to the hippocampal long axis
        (~97 mm through-plane coverage)
  BOLD  9 runs, TR = [TODO]
  fmap  reverse-phase-encode (dir-PA) spin-echo EPI for susceptibility
        distortion correction

Scanner: [TODO — model, field strength, head coil]

## Raw data

Standard BIDS layout under `sub-<label>/`:

  anat/   T1w, T2w
  func/   9 runs of BOLD, with events.tsv and events.json
  fmap/   dir-PA spin-echo EPI

## Derivatives

### derivatives/fmriprep/

Minimally preprocessed anatomical and functional data from fMRIPrep
23.2.1. Two output spaces are provided, both of which are used in the
paper:

  T1w                      participant native anatomical space
  MNI152NLin2009cAsym      volumetric template space

Included per participant: preprocessed T1w and T2w, brain and ribbon
masks, tissue probability maps and discrete segmentation, cortical
surfaces, the transforms between spaces, preprocessed BOLD with
accompanying reference volumes and brain masks, and per-run confound
timeseries.

`t2_to_t1.nii.gz` and `t2_to_t1.mat` are the T2w slab resampled into
native T1w space and the corresponding affine, produced outside fMRIPrep
and used for the hippocampal subfield analyses.

Surface files and `from-fsnative`/`to-fsnative` transforms reference a
FreeSurfer reconstruction that is not distributed here. The surfaces are
self-contained geometry and are usable as provided.

### derivatives/rois/

Only the ROIs used in the analyses reported in the paper.

  ASHS/bin_masks_5B_T1/    hippocampal subfield masks in native T1w space
  ASHS/bin_masks_5B_T2/    the same subfields in native T2w slab space

    Automatic Segmentation of Hippocampal Subfields, 5B atlas, run on the
    non-defaced T1w and T2w. Files are named `combined_<label>_mask_T*`
    for the binarized mask and `combined_<label>_mask_T*_non-bin` for the
    unthresholded version. Labels: CA1, CA2+3, DG, EC, HPC, PHC, PRC,
    Subiculum.

    ASHS requires whole-head, non-skull-stripped anatomicals and so
    cannot be reproduced from the defaced T1w distributed here. The
    resulting masks are therefore shipped directly.

  harvard-oxford/          four cortical ROIs warped to native T1w space,
                           from the FSL Harvard-Oxford atlas:
                             Heschl's Gyrus (includes H1 and H2)
                             Superior Temporal Gyrus, posterior division
                             Lateral Occipital Cortex, inferior division
                             Occipital Pole

  antpost-segs/            anterior and posterior hippocampal divisions
                           (`*_rd_q`), left, right, and combined

## Defacing and privacy

The raw T1w images were defaced with pydeface. Visual inspection of
mid-sagittal, off-midline, and maximum-intensity-projection views
confirmed removal of facial features with no loss of brain tissue.

No other image in this dataset carries identifiable facial anatomy:

  - The T2w scans are limited-FOV oblique slabs centered on the
    hippocampus, with ~97 mm through-plane coverage and no facial
    structure. They are distributed as acquired.
  - All fMRIPrep anatomical outputs are brain-extracted.
  - All ROI masks are brain-derived.

Accordingly, defacing was applied only where a face was present. JSON
sidecars were stripped of acquisition dates and times, institution and
station identifiers, device serial numbers, and operator or patient
fields. Gzip headers were written without embedded filenames or
timestamps.

## Known issues

  - sub-mm04 and sub-mm12 show excessive head motion and are excluded
    from the analyses in the paper. See `participants.tsv`.
  - FreeSurfer reconstructions and ASHS intermediate files
    (multiatlas/, bootstrap/) are not distributed.

